A fire's size is taken from \code{SitesChecked}. The published extension logs \code{DamagedSites} as one
more than the cells the fire burned, on every event, while \code{SitesChecked} equals the burned
cells on the timestep's severity map. \code{MeanSeverity} is divided by that inflated count, so the
returned \code{mean_severity} is rescaled to \code{MeanSeverity * DamagedSites / SitesChecked}, the mean
over the burned cells. A build with the count fixed logs \code{DamagedSites == SitesChecked}, which
the same rescaling leaves unchanged. A log that holds neither relation on every event warns.

Cells -> hectares uses \code{pixel_area_ha} (1 ha for a 100 m x 100 m grid).

The \code{area_by_fuel_ha} summary is computed by intersecting the per-timestep
Dynamic Fire severity rasters (cells with severity > 1 = burned; values 0
and 1 are inactive and active-but-unburned respectively) with the matching
per-timestep Dynamic Fuels \code{FuelType} rasters from the same
\verb{<rep_dir>/fire/} subdirectory. Cell-fuel-timestep is the unit of
accounting: a cell that burns in two distinct timesteps contributes twice
(consistent with NBAC's per-fire-perimeter accounting on the observed side).
This replaces the earlier convention of attributing each event's
entire \code{DamagedSites} count to its \code{InitFuel}, which biased simulated
burn area toward the dominant-cover fuel since fires ignite where there's
igniteable fuel and then spread anywhere.

Dynamic Fuels writes each active cell of a \code{FuelType} map as its fuel-type
index plus one, and an inactive cell as 0. \code{fuel_code} is that raw map
value, \code{fuel_index} is \code{fuel_code - 1} (0 for an active cell with no fuel
type), and \code{base} is the base fuel type of that index in the run's own
\code{FuelTypeTable}, read from \verb{<rep_dir>/dynamic-fire.txt}. Index 0, and an
index the table does not list, have no base (\code{NA}) and are not scored; the
latter also warns. If the file or its \code{FuelTypeTable} is missing,
\code{\link[=defaultFuelTypeTable]{defaultFuelTypeTable()}} is used instead, with a warning.
